<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar</h1><p class="st-meta">1572 CE – 1741 CE · capital Weimar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ernestine Saxon duchy centred on Weimar, forerunner of the Goethe-era duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Ernestine Wettins</a>, who lost the electorate in 1547, kept lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> that were repeatedly divided. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt-division">Erfurt Division</a> of 1572 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-duke-of-saxe-weimar">Duke Frederick William I</a> the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, while his cousins received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar's</a> Lutheran dukes ruled a farming territory with the university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> nearby. The duchy was repeatedly divided among brothers, and in 1640 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a> split off, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-of-saxe-weimar">Bernhard of Saxe-Weimar</a> became a famous Protestant general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> served as court organist and concertmaster at Weimar from 1708 to 1717.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach</a>'s line died out in 1741, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-i-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duke Ernest Augustus</a> I united the two as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</a>. Under Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-amalia-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Anna Amalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-augustus-grand-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Charles Augustus</a> the united duchy became the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Goethe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Schiller</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-weimar.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Weimar" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Simsunman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SajoniaWeimar_Map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1572 CE – 1741 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Weimar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick William I, William, Ernest Augustus I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Weimar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1656&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-weimar" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Weimar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4937 410 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Weimar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5257 666L5229 694M5257 666L5257 666M5257 666L5235 666M5257 666L5263 667M5257 666L5244 667"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5257 666h0M5235 666h0M5263 667h0M5244 667h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5257 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5257 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Weimar. <a href="/atlas?year=1656&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-weimar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
